The Moon's [[axis|axis]] is tilted only slightly, so near its poles, the Sun always sits low on the horizon. Some craters there are so deep that their floors have stayed in shadow for billions of years. NASA's Lunar Reconnaissance Orbiter measured temperatures in some of them below −240 °C. That is colder than the surface of [[@pluto|Pluto]], and among the coldest places known in the [[solar-system|solar system]].

Any water that drifts into these craters freezes and stays. Instruments suggest ice is mixed into the soil there, but nobody knows yet whether it forms thick layers or is scattered thinly.

{{story:water-cycle}}

In April 2001, Dennis Tito rode a Russian Soyuz rocket to the space station and spent about a week there. NASA wasn't happy at first, but Russia needed the money. A handful of others followed over the next decade, each paying millions. They included Anousheh Ansari, an Iranian-American engineer who in 2006 became the first woman to fly as a paying space tourist.

The real change came when private companies started building their own spacecraft. Making rockets that land and fly again, instead of falling into the sea, cut costs hugely. That is the story of [[@reusable-rockets|reusable rockets]].

{{story:reusable-rockets}}

भारत और अमेरिका के बहुत-से बच्चे [[@aesop|ईसप]] की प्यासे कौए वाली कहानी पढ़ते हैं। कौआ घड़े में कंकड़ डालता जाता है, जब तक पानी इतना ऊपर न आ जाए कि वह पी सके। 2014 में कैम्ब्रिज विश्वविद्यालय और न्यूज़ीलैंड के वैज्ञानिकों ने न्यू कैलेडोनिया के कौओं के साथ इसे परखा। उन्होंने पानी से भरी एक नली में तैरता हुआ खाना रखा, ठीक इतना नीचे कि चोंच न पहुँचे।

कौओं ने पानी ऊपर लाने के लिए कंकड़ डाले। चुनने का मौका मिला, तो उन्होंने तैरने वाली हल्की चीज़ों की बजाय भारी पत्थर चुने। उन्होंने रेत वाली नली की जगह पानी वाली नली चुनी। उनके नतीजे लगभग पाँच से सात साल के बच्चों जैसे थे।

{{story:octopus-smart}}

A humpback song has a structure, a bit like a [[@music-notes|piece of music]]. Short sounds form phrases. Repeated phrases form themes. Several themes, sung in a set order, make up the song. When one song ends, the whale usually starts again from the beginning.

All the males in a population sing roughly the same song. Over the season, they change it bit by bit, adding new phrases and dropping old ones, and they all change in step. Each year's song is a little different from the last. Humpbacks are among the few animals besides people known to keep changing a learned song like this.

{{story:voyager}}

When an elephant herd finds a field of ripe rice, sugarcane or bananas, it's like finding a buffet. A single night can wipe out a farmer's crop for the whole season. Farmers try to drive elephants away with shouting, drums, firecrackers and torches, and sometimes with dangerous illegal electric fences.

Frightened or chased elephants can charge. Many deaths happen in the dark, when people walking along paths or through fields don't see a herd until it is too close. Government figures show more than 2,500 people died this way between 2019 and 2024. In the same years, hundreds of elephants died from electrocution, train collisions and poisoning.

{{story:elephants-talk}}

A water jet usually breaks into drops quickly. The archerfish has a neat trick. It squeezes the water out slowly at first and faster at the end. The back of the jet catches up with the front, forming a blob of water right at the moment it reaches the target.

So the fish isn't throwing a thin stream, but more like a water bullet that bunches up mid-air. Engineers have studied this as a way to send liquid precisely over a distance. Its sea cousin, the [[@power-pistol-shrimp|pistol shrimp]], uses water as a weapon in a very different way.

{{story:power-pistol-shrimp}}

In 1896, the American inventor Jesse Reno built a moving ramp at Coney Island, an amusement park in [[@new-york|New York]]. It was a sloping belt with ridges, not steps, and people rode it for fun. Around the same time, Charles Seeberger designed a moving staircase with flat steps. He worked with the Otis company, which also made [[@elevators|lifts]], and their machine was shown at the great [[@paris|Paris]] Exhibition of 1900.

Seeberger named it the "escalator", from *scala*, the Latin word for steps. The name became so common that in 1950 the US Patent Office ruled that Otis could no longer own it.

{{story:elevators}}

Light from [[@lightning|lightning]] reaches you almost instantly, but its thunder plods along at sound speed. Count the seconds between the flash and the rumble, then divide by three. That's roughly how many kilometres away the storm is.

Sound is faster in water and fastest in solids. [[@whales-india|Whales can hear each other's calls]] across hundreds of kilometres of ocean. [[@whale-songs|Whale songs]] travel especially far through a layer of water where sound bounces along without spreading out much.

{{story:whale-songs}}

Before springs, clocks were driven by heavy hanging weights, so they had to stand still. In the 1400s and 1500s, clockmakers began using a tightly wound coil called a mainspring. As it slowly unwound, it drove the gears. Clocks could finally be small enough to carry.

Around 1675, Hooke and the Dutch scientist Christiaan Huygens each worked on a tiny hairspring that made a watch's balance wheel swing back and forth at a steady beat. This made watches far more accurate. A wind-up watch today still uses a mainspring and a hairspring, with no battery at all, and [[@clocks|clocks]] of this kind are still made by hand.

{{story:toys-history}}

On 8 November 1895, the German physicist Wilhelm Röntgen was testing a glass tube with electricity running through it in Würzburg. He had covered the tube in black card, yet a screen across the room began to glow. Something invisible was escaping the tube and passing through the card.

He called it "X" [[radiation|radiation]], because X stands for something unknown. A few weeks later, he made a picture of his wife Anna Bertha's hand, with the bones and her ring clearly visible. Newspapers printed it and the news spread around the world within weeks. Röntgen refused to patent his discovery, so anyone could build an X-ray machine. In 1901, Röntgen received the first ever Nobel Prize in Physics.

{{story:marie-curie}}

1905 में ब्रिटिश सरकार ने बंगाल को दो हिस्सों में बाँट दिया। बहुत-से बंगालियों ने इसे उन्हें बाँटकर कमज़ोर करने की चाल माना, और [[@swadeshi|स्वदेशी]] आंदोलन शुरू हुआ। लोगों ने अंग्रेज़ी सामान खरीदना बंद किया और भारतीय कपड़ा पहनने लगे।

उसी साल अवनींद्रनाथ ने केसरिया वस्त्र पहने, चार भुजाओं वाली एक युवती का चित्र बनाया, जो हल्के आसमान के नीचे हरी ज़मीन पर खड़ी है। वह एक किताब, धान की बालियों का गुच्छा, सफ़ेद कपड़ा और जप की माला थामे है। पहले इसे बंग माता कहा गया, फिर यह चित्र भारत माता के नाम से मशहूर हुआ। यह छोटा, शांत और लगभग सपने जैसा है, फिर भी इसे जुलूसों में उठाकर ले जाया गया और खूब छापा गया।

{{story:swadeshi}}

Players can't hit another player's stick, obstruct an opponent with their body or lift the ball dangerously. When a foul happens outside the circle, the other team gets a free hit.

Umpires show cards for bad fouls. A green card sends a player off for two minutes. A yellow card means at least five minutes off. A red card means the player is out for the rest of the match. Teams play a player short while someone is off, much like in ice hockey.

{{story:dhyan-chand}}

प्रोटीन छोटे टुकड़ों से बनी लंबी ज़ंजीरें हैं, कुछ-कुछ धागे में पिरोए मनकों की तरह। इन टुकड़ों को अमीनो एसिड कहते हैं। ये 20 तरह के होते हैं। इनमें से 11 शरीर खुद बना सकता है। बाकी 9 खाने से ही आने चाहिए।

दूध और अंडे जैसे जानवरों से मिलने वाले खाने में सभी 9 अच्छी मात्रा में होते हैं। बहुत-से पौधों से मिलने वाले खाने में एक-दो कम होते हैं, पर अलग-अलग पौधे एक-दूसरे की कमी पूरी कर देते हैं। चावल में एक अमीनो एसिड कम होता है जो [[@pulses-dal|दाल]] में भरपूर है, और दाल में एक कम होता है जो चावल में है। इसलिए रोज़ की दाल-चावल की थाली, या मेक्सिको और अमेरिका के दक्षिणी हिस्से में चावल और बीन्स, पूरा सेट दे देती है। परिवारों ने यह बात स्वाद और आदत से वैज्ञानिकों से बहुत पहले समझ ली थी।

{{story:pulses-dal}}

अलग-अलग जुड़वाँ की दर दुनिया भर में बदलती है। यह मध्य अफ़्रीका के कुछ हिस्सों में, और [[@nigeria|नाइजीरिया]] के योरूबा लोगों में सबसे ज़्यादा है। नाइजीरिया का कस्बा इग्बो-ओरा खुद को दुनिया की जुड़वाँ राजधानी कहता है। पूर्वी एशिया में अलग-अलग जुड़वाँ कम होते हैं।

भारत में [[@kerala|केरल]] के मलप्पुरम ज़िले का गाँव कोडिन्ही आम से कहीं ज़्यादा जुड़वाँ होने के लिए मशहूर हो गया है। वैज्ञानिक इसका अध्ययन करने वहाँ गए हैं, पर अभी तक कोई नहीं जानता कि ऐसा क्यों है। हूबहू जुड़वाँ की बात अलग है: वे हर जगह लगभग एक ही दर पर होते हैं, हर 1,000 जन्मों में मोटे तौर पर 3 या 4।

{{story:kerala}}

When the iPhone launched in 2007, many apps copied real objects. The notes app looked like a yellow notepad. The calendar had stitched leather edges. This style is called skeuomorphism, from Greek words meaning "tool" and "shape". It helped people who had never used a [[@touchscreens|touchscreen]] guess what to do.

By the 2010s, people knew how phones worked, and the fake leather started to feel old-fashioned. Microsoft's Windows Phone, in 2010, used bold flat colours and simple tiles. Apple switched to a flat style with iOS 7 in 2013. Today most apps sit somewhere in between, with flat colours plus soft shadows to show what can be pressed.

{{story:design-everyday}}

A few princely states were well ahead of British India in some ways. Mysore built one of Asia's early [[hydroelectric|hydroelectric]] power stations at Shivanasamudra in 1902, and the Krishna Raja Sagara dam on the Kaveri.

In Baroda, Maharaja Sayajirao Gaekwad III made primary education free and compulsory in the early 1900s. He also gave a scholarship to a brilliant young student named B.R. [[@ambedkar|Ambedkar]], who went to Columbia University in New York and later led the drafting of India's Constitution. In 1936 Travancore's ruler opened its temples to people of all castes, a major step against untouchability.

{{story:mysuru}}

West of the [[@mississippi|Mississippi River]] come the Great Plains. For more than 1,000 km the land is almost flat and slowly rises towards the west. Huge herds of bison once roamed here. Today much of it is farmland growing wheat and maize, which is why it is sometimes called America's breadbasket.

The plains are also where warm, wet air from the Gulf of Mexico meets cold, dry air from Canada. When they clash in spring, they can spin up [[@tornadoes|tornadoes]], so often that one area is nicknamed Tornado Alley.

{{story:grand-canyon}}

The first 29 Navajo recruits created the code themselves at a Marine camp in California. They kept their messages short and made two layers.

The first layer was an alphabet. Each English letter had one or more Navajo words standing for it: "wol-la-chee" (ant) meant A. The second layer was a list of about 200 military words, later grown to over 400. Birds stood for aircraft and fish for ships. A bomber was a buzzard. Every code talker learned it all by heart, because a written copy could be captured. Common letters such as E, T and A were given several different Navajo words, so a listener could not spot a word repeating and guess what it meant.

{{story:cipher-codes}}

The early Chinese wrote on [[@bamboo|bamboo]] strips, which were heavy, and on silk, which was expensive. Paper was light and cheap. According to an official history written centuries later, Cai Lun presented his papermaking method to the Emperor He in 105 CE, and the emperor praised it.

Chinese papermakers kept improving their craft. Paper was used for books, letters, wrapping, umbrellas, kites and even clothing. By about the 700s CE, the Chinese were using woodblock printing on paper, and later they made the first paper money.

{{story:ancient-china}}

About 2,000 years ago, Roman ships sailed to the Malabar coast every year, riding the [[@monsoon|monsoon]] winds. One of their ports was Muziris, close to today's [[@kochi|Kochi]]. They paid in gold and silver coins, and hoards of Roman coins have been dug up in south India.

The Roman writer Pliny the Elder grumbled that India, China and Arabia took millions of coins from the [[empire|empire]] every year. In Rome, pepper was kept in special warehouses. Even today in England, a "peppercorn rent" means a tiny, symbolic rent, from the days when a few peppercorns had real value.

{{story:columbus}}

In British India, the government held a [[monopoly|monopoly]] on salt. Indians were not allowed to make or sell it freely, and they paid a tax on every bit they bought. Because everyone needs salt, the tax hit the poorest hardest.

On 12 March 1930, [[@gandhi|Mahatma Gandhi]] set out from his ashram near Ahmedabad with 78 followers. They walked for 24 days, about 385 km, to the coastal village of Dandi. On 6 April, Gandhi picked up a lump of natural salt from the shore, breaking the law. Across India, thousands followed his lead, and tens of thousands were arrested.

{{story:salt-march}}

Pepper vines climb trees in the [[@forests-india|wet forests]] of the [[@western-ghats|Western Ghats]]. Two thousand years ago, Roman traders sailed to the port of Muziris, near today's [[@kochi|Kochi]], to buy pepper. Hoards of Roman gold coins have been dug up in South India.

Pepper was so prized in Europe that it was sometimes used to pay rent. Arab and Indian merchants controlled the sea trade for centuries. In 1498, the Portuguese sailor Vasco da Gama reached Kozhikode (Calicut), and European powers began a long and often violent struggle to control the spice trade. Christopher [[@columbus|Columbus]] had set out a few years earlier, sailing west, hoping to reach the same riches.

{{story:columbus}}
